WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.930
It is Thursday, which means we have the latest
unemployment report from the previous week.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.499
The numbers are staggering.

00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:12.750
Once again, an additional 4.4 million new
people filed for unemployment benefits last

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:13.750
week.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.420
These are what we call initial claims when
you first filed for benefits.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.010
Remember that we get the numbers on Thursday
from the previous week.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.810
So let's look at the total numbers since the
start of the Corona virus pandemic.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:31.220
Remember that before the pandemic, the most
jobless claims we had had in one week was

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.609
in 1982, I believe, and it was somewhere between
six and eight hundred thousand.

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:39.320
I don't remember the numbers exactly, but
compare them to the numbers from the last

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.420
five weeks.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:47.239
The week ending March 21, 3.3 million new
jobless claims week ending March 28.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:49.000
6.8 million.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.760
April 4th. 6.6 million.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.819
April eleven.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.859
Another 5.2 million.

00:00:54.859 --> 00:00:58.999
And then the week ending April 18, 4.4 million.

00:00:58.999 --> 00:01:05.710
So this is now a total of twenty six point
four million people who have filed for unemployment

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.850
since the start of the corona virus pandemic.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.520
We believed that the labor force was about
158 million right before this started.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:20.430
So what this means is that nearly 17 percent
of the country has lost their jobs in the

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.420
last five weeks.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.689
So a few different things.

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:29.670
First of all, the scope and magnitude of this,
these are job loss numbers that would have

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.289
been inconceivable four months ago or four
months ago.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:38.149
Someone said how would the economy react to
17 percent of the country losing their jobs?

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:39.670
It's cartoonish.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.039
These are cartoonishly huge numbers.

00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:47.710
No one alive today lived through anything
like this in their working lives.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.710
OK.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:54.429
We have now lost more jobs in the last five
weeks than the total number of jobs created

00:01:54.429 --> 00:01:55.759
since the Great Recession.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:58.189
OK, so let me clarify those numbers.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:05.939
During the 2008 recession, we lost 8.7 million
jobs since we started recovering from that

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:06.939
recession.

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:10.690
Twenty two point four million jobs were created
total since 2009.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:16.340
OK, we now have lost more than 26 million
in the last five weeks.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:22.030
The entire jobs recovery from the recession,
including the jobs created under Barack Obama

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.870
and the roughly 6 million that were created
under Donald Trump up until this started.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.950
All of those jobs are gone.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.950
So that's number one.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:30.950
The magnitude of it.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.220
Number two, every one of us that has a job
should be very grateful because the situation

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.410
is truly horrifying, that that's just an interjection.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.590
Even people who have lost jobs and want to
go on unemployment insurance are having trouble.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:45.770
So it's not as simple as well.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.710
You lose your job, the government's going
to bail you out.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.900
There are states that may soon run out of
unemployment money.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.660
Many people just aren't hearing back for weeks.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.130
Many people filed or attempted to file weeks
ago.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:01.080
They haven't gotten a dollar yet because the
systems are so overwhelmed, truly dystopian.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.730
Now, in terms of the outlook, even if new
claims slow down and they probably will.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:06.730
Right.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:11.010
So we may go down to only 3.8 million new
claims next week and maybe 3.4 the fault,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.010
right?

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.650
You could have a declining growth rate.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.760
The total number of people on unemployment
could continue climbing in total over the

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.120
next few months.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.260
Depending on the spread of the pandemic.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.260
We could have 30 million.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.360
We could have 35 million.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.140
I spoke to one economist who thinks by July,
depending on how things go, we could have

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.020
40 million people on unemployment benefits.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.720
Can the states even afford that?

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:31.750
It's not clear.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:37.110
Now you have a contingent from the right that
says just open it up and it will all get resolved

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.190
because the economy will restart.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:43.030
Doesn't seem to be the case because unless
and until you deal with the pandemic, when

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.190
you you can open a restaurant, you can open
a barbershop, you can open a nail salon.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.970
Doesn't mean that people are going to go.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:50.810
It's not going to fix this.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:57.220
If here in Massachusetts right now, salons
and barber shops opened up, I'm not going

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.350
to get a haircut.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.970
Nobody I've spoken to is going to get a haircut.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:06.170
What good is taking someone off of unemployment
benefits so that they can stand in a barbershop,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.300
clippers and scissors in hand with no customers
coming in?

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.630
So with history as our guide, we know that
the one way to deal with this is to deal with

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.800
the virus itself as quickly as possible.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:20.350
Now there's new research, there's new studies
that are suggesting that lockdown down or

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:26.400
not, the curve is roughly similar in in growth
rate, not in absolute numbers.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.040
And that is something we should be thinking
about.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.500
I'm going to be studying that tomorrow, over
the weekend.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.970
And I'll I'll come to you with information
about that on Monday.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.500
But opening business is just so people can
stand there without customers is not a path.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.500
It's not a strategy.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.080
It's not really anything.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.720
States can't really open economies.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.130
People can open economies.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:50.440
And despite the Astroturf protests, which
we'll talk about later today, most people

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:55.030
right now aren't willing to go and pretend
everything is normal, even if a bunch of businesses

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:56.090
start opening up.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:22.870
Very important

00:05:22.870 --> 00:06:06.110
to understand that.

